<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=abacus">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Writing and counting</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abacus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 2700–2300 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/28th-century-bce">28th century BCE</a> · Also known as Counting frame</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">An abacus, or counting frame, is a hand-operated calculating tool of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bead">beads</a> or counters moved on a board or rods, first known in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumer">Sumer</a>, where it appeared between 2700 and 2300 BCE with columns for the orders of magnitude of the base-60 number system. The marble Salamis Tablet of 300 BCE is the oldest counting board yet discovered. Greek, Roman and Chinese forms followed, and abacuses remained in everyday use until handheld electronic calculators largely replaced them in the 1980s.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abacus.jpg" alt="Abacus" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: User Tor Svensson · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kulram.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>An abacus is a two-dimensional array of slidable <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bead">beads</a> or similar objects. In the earliest designs the beads lay loose on a flat surface or slid in grooves, and later they were made to slide on rods in a frame, which allowed faster handling. Each rod usually stands for one digit of a number in a positional system. Roman and East Asian abacuses resemble bi-quinary coded decimal, with a top deck of one or two beads for fives and a bottom deck of four or five beads for ones. One abacus supports several methods of addition, subtraction, multiplication, division and square and cube roots. The word is first recorded in Middle English in 1387, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>, which took it from Greek abax.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The earliest evidence for the Greek abacus dates to the fifth century BCE, and a table of wood or marble with small counters spread through Achaemenid Persia, the Etruscan world and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. Writing in the first century BCE, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horace">Horace</a> refers to a wax abacus. A Roman example of the first century CE has eight long grooves with up to five <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bead">beads</a> each, marked from units up to millions, and eight shorter grooves for fives. The Roman system of counter casting was widely used in medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and wealthy users had decorative minted counters called jetons.</p><p>The earliest written mention of the Chinese abacus, the suanpan, dates to the second century BCE. A painting of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song dynasty</a>, Along the River During the Qingming Festival, shows one beside an account book on an apothecary's counter. The soroban was brought to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> in the fourteenth century, and the abacus reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> around 1400.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-sylvester-ii">Pope Sylvester II</a> reintroduced the abacus with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bead">beads</a> on wires, so that it came into wide use again in eleventh-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. It was widely used there as late as the seventeenth century, until decimal notation and written methods of calculation replaced it. The Russian abacus, the schoty, has a slanted deck with ten beads on each wire, one wire having four beads for quarter-rubles, and was used in shops and markets until the 1990s. The mathematician Jean-Victor Poncelet, who had been a prisoner of war in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, brought it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> around 1820 as a teaching aid.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Abacus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>2700–2300 BCE</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Sumer</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd><a href="/encyclopedia/sumer">Sumer</a></dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>The form of the Sumerian device is unknown; the oldest surviving counting board, the Salamis Tablet, dates from about 300 BCE.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/sexagesimal">Base-60 numbers<span>3rd millennium BCE</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=abacus">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
